# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""011.md batch M: rewrite vv 120, 121, 122, 123 (template filler). Completes 011.md.

Translations already correct; commentary only (translation=None).

Source apparatus:
  v120 -- [120]: the stories of the previous generations were revealed to the Prophet
      in order to make firm his heart, i.e. to strengthen and console him in the face
      of the difficulties of his mission; see also 11:112-13c. The Quran's accounts of
      sacred history are often read as spiritual allegories teaching human beings how
      to overcome the enemies within their own souls. On the reminder, see 51:55:
      'And remind, for truly the reminder benefits the believers.'
      [112-13]: 'Be steadfast' understood by some to mean hold steadfastly to the
      Quran (R); by extension also directed to those who turn in repentance with him,
      the Prophet's Companions (Q). 'Incline not toward the wrongdoers' = believers
      should never approve of the actions of the Makkan idolaters (IK).
  v121-22 -- [121-22]: these statements function as warnings and threats against the
      Makkan idolaters; see 6:135c. There: 'Work in accordance with your ability' =
      continue with what you have been doing, implying the futility of such actions;
      'not meant as a license, but as a warning and a threat' (T), for the Prophet is
      also working to fulfil the Divine Command. The Prophet is elsewhere instructed to
      tell the disbelievers to wait, as he too is waiting (9:52; 10:20, 102). In the end
      it will be seen which of the two has his end in the Abode. The end belongs to the
      reverent (7:128; 11:49; 13:35; 28:83).
  v123 -- [123]: the surah ends with a summary of its message to the Prophet and, by
      extension, to all believers. The call to trust is echoed at 3:159: 'Truly God
      loves those who trust'; and 5:23: 'And trust in God, if you are believers.' Sufi
      interpretation of trust: trust is there when 'the heart is at rest because of the
      Lord's Guarantee' (Aj).
"""

SURAH = "Hūd"
NUM = 11

SECTIONS = {

120: (None, [
 ("That Whereby We Make Firm Thine Heart", [
  "All that We recount unto thee of the stories of the messengers is that whereby We make firm thine heart. The verse states the purpose of everything the sūrah has narrated, and the purpose is not informational. The stories were revealed to strengthen and console the Prophet in the face of the difficulties he encountered in his prophetic mission.",
  "The commentators connect this to 11:112–13, where the command be steadfast is understood by some to mean that he should hold steadfastly to the Qur'an (R), and by extension is directed also toward those who turn in repentance along with him — the Prophet's Companions (Q). The steadiness commanded there is what the narratives here supply the grounds for. The sūrah is telling its recipient that he is not the first to be opposed, and that the outcome of that opposition is on record."]),
 ("The Consolation Is in the Pattern", [
  "What consoles is not that previous prophets suffered but that they prevailed. The sūrah has narrated five complete cycles in which the messenger was opposed, mocked, and threatened, and in every case the community that opposed him was destroyed while he and the believers were saved. The pattern is the consolation.",
  "The detail matters. Noah was mocked by the notables while he built the ark, and the water came. Hūd was told he was deluded, and the wind came. Ṣāliḥ was defied to bring a sign, and the cry came. Lot was threatened with expulsion, and the stones came. Shuʿayb was told he would be stoned, and the cry came. In each case the threat against the prophet was not carried out and the threat he carried was. The commentators' note that the Prophet is granted assurance of ultimate triumph rests on this record rather than on a promise detached from it.",
  "The commentators note that the assurance granted here is that, like the other prophets before him, the Prophet and his followers will ultimately triumph over their enemies, and that the latter will suffer a humiliating defeat if they do not mend their ways. The verse does not state this directly; it states that the stories make the heart firm, and leaves the reader to see why they would."]),
 ("An Exhortation and a Reminder", [
  "And herein there has come unto thee the Truth, and an exhortation and a reminder for the believers. Three things are named: the Truth, an exhortation, and a reminder. The commentators connect the last to 51:55: And remind, for truly the reminder benefits the believers.",
  "The qualification matters. A reminder benefits those already disposed to remember; it does not create the disposition. This is the same distinction the sūrah drew at v103, where the sign was a sign for those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter. The narratives are available to everyone and function for those already inclined. The verse says so by attaching the reminder to the believers rather than to mankind in general."]),
 ("Read as Allegory", [
  "The commentators add a further layer: the Qur'an's accounts of episodes in sacred history are often read as spiritual allegories that teach human beings in general how to overcome the enemies within their own souls. On this reading the destroyed towns are not only historical communities but representations of what must be overcome in the self.",
  "The commentators present this as a common reading rather than as the verse's explicit claim, and the verse does not require it. What the allegorical reading preserves is the applicability of the narratives to a reader who is not a prophet and not a Makkah. The stories were revealed to make one man's heart firm; the commentators note that they have been read as doing the same work for others."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say that the stories are exhaustive, and does not claim to recount everything the messengers did. It says all that We recount unto thee — limiting itself to what has been recounted. The sūrah elsewhere acknowledges that messengers were sent whose stories are not told.",
  "The verse also does not say that the firmness is permanent or that it removes the difficulty. The heart is made firm in the face of difficulties, not delivered from them. The commentators' gloss — to strengthen and console him in the face of the difficulties he encountered — keeps the difficulty in place and describes what the stories do within it."]),
]),

121: (None, [
 ("Act According to Your Position", [
  "And say unto those who believe not, Act according to your position; we, too, are acting. The verse instructs the Prophet to address the disbelievers directly, and the address is not an argument. It is a statement that both parties will continue as they are.",
  "The commentators note that these statements function as warnings and threats against the Makkan idolaters, and direct the reader to 6:135, where the parallel instruction is given: Say, O my people! Work in accordance with your ability; I, too, am working. Soon you shall know whose end is the Abode."]),
 ("Not a Licence", [
  "The commentators are explicit that the instruction is not permissive. At 6:135 they explain that the Prophet is instructed to tell the disbelievers to work in accordance with their ability — that is, to continue with what they have been doing, including their opposition to the Qur'anic message, to the best of their ability — implying the futility of such actions. The statement is therefore not meant as a licence but as a warning and a threat (Ṭ), for the Prophet is also working to fulfil the Divine Command and the mission he has been given.",
  "This reading settles what might otherwise look like indifference. The verse is not saying that both courses are acceptable and each party may choose. It is saying that the disbelievers may continue, and that continuing will not succeed. The permission is rhetorical, and its function is to make clear that no further argument is being offered."]),
 ("We, Too, Are Acting", [
  "The second half of the instruction is the verse's counterweight. The disbelievers act according to their position, and the believers act according to theirs. The parallelism is exact and the difference is in the position, not in the activity.",
  "The commentators note at 6:135 that the Prophet is also working — to fulfil the Divine Command and the mission he has been given. The two activities are therefore not equivalent in kind. One is persistence in opposition; the other is fulfilment of a commission. The verse puts them side by side without commenting on the asymmetry, and the following verse supplies the outcome.",
  "The phrase act according to your position is worth holding against the sūrah's earlier vocabulary. Shuʿayb said at v88: I desire not to do behind your backs that which I forbid you; I desire only reform so far as I am able. He described his own action in terms of capacity and restraint. Here the disbelievers are told to act according to their position, which is a different register — not capacity but standing, not restraint but persistence. The verse gives them the grammatical form the prophets used for themselves and fills it with opposite content."]),
 ("The Function of the Address", [
  "The verse comes after the narratives and after the assurance at v120. Its placement makes it a conclusion rather than an opening. The arguments have been made, the examples have been given, and the instruction now is to state the position and stop.",
  "The commentators' note that these are warnings and threats against the Makkan idolaters identifies the audience as historical and specific. The verse is addressed to a situation, and its force depends on that situation: a prophet who has been opposed for years, speaking to opponents who have not been persuaded, saying that both sides will continue and that the continuation will decide."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say what the believers' position is. It says we, too, are acting, and leaves the content of the action to what has preceded. It does not describe the disbelievers' position either, beyond identifying them as those who believe not.",
  "The verse also does not say that the disbelievers are incapable of changing. It instructs the Prophet to tell them to act according to their position, which describes what they are doing rather than fixing what they must do. The sūrah has repeatedly held out the possibility of reform — at v117 most explicitly — and this verse does not withdraw it. It states the present position and moves on."]),
]),

122: (None, [
 ("And Wait", [
  "And wait! We, too, are waiting. The verse completes the instruction begun at v121 and reduces it to a single verb. What the disbelievers are told to do is wait, and what the believers are doing is the same.",
  "The commentators note that the Prophet is elsewhere instructed to tell the disbelievers to wait, as he too is waiting, and cite 9:52 and 10:20 and 102. The recurrence shows that this is a settled form of address rather than a local device. Wherever the Qur'an reaches the point at which argument is exhausted and only the outcome remains, this is the form it takes."]),
 ("What Is Being Waited For", [
  "Neither party is waiting for nothing. The verse's context supplies the object: at 6:135 the parallel instruction continues, Soon you shall know whose end is the Abode, and the commentators there explain that in the end it will be seen which of the two has his end in the Abode — that is, in the permanent Abode of the Hereafter.",
  "The waiting is therefore directed at a determination. Two courses are being pursued, and the outcome will show which was right. The verse does not argue the point further because the argument has been made; what remains is the event that will settle it. The commentators note elsewhere that the end belongs to the reverent, citing 7:128, 11:49, 13:35, and 28:83 — and 11:49 is within this very sūrah.",
  "That the sūrah cites itself here is not incidental. Verse 49 closes the account of Noah with the same assurance: So be patient. Truly the end belongs to the reverent. The waiting named at v122 rests on a promise the sūrah has already made once, and the cross-reference ties the present instruction to that earlier one.",
  "Verse 49 is also where the sūrah first names what v123 will make its subject: These are among the accounts of the Unseen that We reveal unto thee. Thou knewest not of them, neither thou nor thy people, beforehand (Kl, Z note that the Prophet did not know such accounts before the revelation came, nor were they known to the people of Makkah). The sūrah opens its claim about the unseen at v49 and closes it at v123, and the frame is deliberate."]),
 ("The Symmetry and Its Limits", [
  "The symmetry of the verse is formal rather than substantive. Both parties wait, and the waiting is the same activity. What differs is what each is waiting for and what each will receive.",
  "The commentators' treatment of the parallel at 6:135 makes the asymmetry explicit: the disbelievers' actions are futile, and the statement is a threat rather than a licence. The symmetry in the wording is what makes the threat effective. The verse does not say that the believers will win and the disbelievers lose; it says both are waiting, and leaves the reader to recall what the sūrah has already shown about how such waiting ends."]),
 ("The End of the Argument", [
  "This is the last thing the disbelievers are told in the sūrah. After it the address turns to the Prophet alone, and the final verse is directed to him and through him to the believers. The argument with the opponents closes here, and it closes without a final refutation.",
  "The commentators' identification of these verses as warnings and threats explains the closure. A warning does not require the warned party's agreement. The verse states the position, states the waiting, and stops — which is what a warning does when the argument has already been made and refused."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say how long the waiting will last. It does not connect the waiting to the limited term of v104, and does not say whether the determination will come in this world or in the Hereafter — though the parallel at 6:135 names the Abode of the Hereafter as what will be known.",
  "The verse also does not say that the waiting is passive. The instruction at v121 was to act, and the waiting follows from the acting rather than replacing it. Both parties are doing something and both are waiting for the outcome, and the verse does not describe the waiting as inactivity on either side."]),
]),

123: (None, [
 ("Unto God Belongs the Unseen", [
  "Unto God belongs the Unseen in the heavens and on the earth. The verse opens by assigning the unseen to God, and the assignment is exclusive. What is hidden in both realms belongs to Him, and the verse does not qualify the claim or share it.",
  "The commentators treat this as part of the sūrah's summary of its message to the Prophet and, by extension, to all believers. The unseen has been the subject of the sūrah throughout — the punishment the disbelievers denied, the Day they mocked, the outcome of the waiting the previous verse named. The verse states who holds it, and the statement grounds everything the sūrah has claimed about what is coming.",
  "The claim also answers the objection the sūrah has been answering since v8, where the disbelievers asked what kept the punishment back. Their objection was that nothing visible supported the warning. The verse's reply is that what supports it is by definition not visible: the unseen belongs to God, and the fact that it is unseen is not evidence against it. The sūrah ends by naming the category its opponents had been treating as empty."]),
 ("Unto Him Are All Matters Returned", [
  "And unto Him are all matters returned. The verse adds the direction of return to the ownership of the unseen. Matters do not merely belong to God; they come back to Him.",
  "This is the sūrah's conclusion in miniature. The narratives have shown communities destroyed, prophets vindicated, and the disbelievers' plans defeated. What the verse states is that this is not a series of interventions but a structure: all matters return. The commentators note that the sūrah ends with this summary after recounting the fate of the previous generations and granting the Prophet assurance that he and his followers will ultimately triumph."]),
 ("So Worship Him, and Trust in Him", [
  "The two commands follow from the two claims. Because the unseen belongs to God and all matters return to Him, the response is worship and trust. The commentators note that the call to trust is echoed throughout the Qur'an, citing 3:159: Truly God loves those who trust; and 5:23: And trust in God, if you are believers.",
  "The commentators also record a Sufi interpretation of the virtue of trust: trust is there when the heart is at rest because of the Lord's Guarantee (Aj). The definition connects trust directly to the verse's opening claim. If the unseen belongs to God and matters return to Him, then the guarantee is real, and the heart's rest is a response to something known rather than a disposition cultivated in the absence of knowledge."]),
 ("Thy Lord Is Not Heedless", [
  "And thy Lord is not heedless of that which you do. The verse closes with a statement about attention. The Lord is not unaware of what is done, and the sūrah ends on that note rather than on the commands.",
  "The placement is significant. After the ownership of the unseen and the return of all matters, after worship and trust, the final word is that nothing is missed. The sūrah has narrated communities destroyed for what they did and prophets saved through mercy, and the last verse states the condition that made both possible: the deeds were seen. The verse does not say whether the seeing results in reward or punishment, and does not need to — the sūrah has shown both."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not specify what the unseen contains. It assigns it to God without enumerating it, and does not say whether any of it will be disclosed or when.",
  "The verse also does not say what will be done about what is seen. It states that the Lord is not heedless, and leaves the consequence to the sūrah's preceding narratives and to the waiting named at v122. The commentators describe the verse as a summary of the sūrah's message, and a summary does not add new claims — it gathers what has been said. What this one gathers is the ownership of the unseen, the return of all matters, the commands of worship and trust, and the assurance that nothing is overlooked."]),
]),

}
